{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: generic MCP Draft4 at 9aa8fccdd66ee518082468bad526c2411048c9be, tree767e096408dc09a5afd4e4bd3dd09075fb6b3507. Preserve Hugh's dynamic act tools, the full four claims and the adopted775 outcomes. The seven prior corrections are materially addressed, but these remaining contradictions prevent approval:\n\n1. P2 — Separate preparation-step outcomes from final act submission (plan031:136,560–601). Native preparation does not consume the intent key; its retry identity is intent digest/capability/step. The new prepare tool shares final-act custody and its terminal accepted/refused table, so an accepted preparation receipt can stop the subsequent act from being submitted, and a second step under the same envelope has no distinct lifecycle. Native foreign steps also prepare at a separate resource-owner scope, while the plan requires the same target. Keep one exact signed intent and beside envelope, but specify independently recorded per-resource/capability/step requests, answers and unknown recovery versus final-act phase and settlement. W2/W6 must drive two distinct steps, same-step retry, lost preparation reply, foreign-owner preparation, then actual act submission and independent settlement. A prep receipt must never be returned as the final act receipt.\n\n2. P2 — Keep valid acts on old pinned lanes (170–176 versus W2:740). The binding rule rejects a lane whose definition was retired, while W2 requires an old lane under that retired definition still served. Retirement from current activation must not invalidate a scope's immutable pinned declaration. Retain the variant while an inventory scope pins it; refuse a descriptor only when its actual name/digest/declaration does not bind the target. Preserve stale-descriptor refusal before signing for genuine mismatch.\n\n3. P2 — Make complete output bounds and costs executable (sections8–9). room's fixed three reads no longer account for standalone roots and separate memberships/session acquisition; tools/list likewise needs each relevant standing read. scope promises live items under8KiB, but a single native non-detached text may reach64KiB. “Paged” needs a concrete complete byte continuation/snapshot contract for mutable summaries and oversized individual items, bound to the observed head. Also prove that every single schema fits a256KiB list page or provide a coherent whole-schema exception. Define bounded segmentation/indexing for arbitrarily many retained resubmission outcomes rather than appending them forever to an at-most2MiB record. None of these bounds may truncate or silently drop valid functionality. W5 should exercise a large single value/schema, mixed memberships and repeated terminal resubmissions, counting every transport/session/fallback read.\n\n4. P2 — Make list generation reflect actual catalog changes (226–239). Hashing whole directory/membership/rules heads makes ordinary work change generation even if declarations and caller standing stay unchanged. That causes notifications and cursor restart inconsistent with the rare semantic changes and W2 “none otherwise” rule. Define the semantic inputs to the actual advertised list, including any description fields that vary, and distinguish observation heads from list generation. Test an ordinary head advance with unchanged catalog and a genuine declaration/standing change. Avoid unnecessary invalidation under sustained multi-agent work.\n\n5. P2/P3 — Reconcile default/generic recipes and real native verbs (450,724,739). Section3 correctly hides acts/act in default mode, but credential next-step and canonical activities still direct them. Give exact recipes and measured call counts for default, --generic replacement and --generic=add, without hidden default calls. W1's register.claim is not the native generated verb: the declaration/CLI use found, hence register.found. Account explicitly for claim's enrollment/confirmation sequence. These are functional recipes, not merely labels.\n\nFurther robustness: the first-eight-digest variant names need deterministic collision handling while retaining full binding checks. This is a secondary design detail, not permission to reduce the dynamic catalog.\n\nArchitecture: Preparation/log/outbox/duty, durable standalone discovery, per-membership unknown standing and mode selection improve completeness. The remaining preparation and old-lane contradictions cut off valid native activities; the output/generation contracts need completion for scalability. The same service must continue to support unfamiliar definitions without per-application code. Do not restore obsolete fixed tool counts or server topology.\n\nSecurity: Exact declaration binding, control/field separation, no generated idempotency key, retained terminal refusal until explicit resubmit, and definitive same-incarnation recovery improve safety. Keep foreign preparation owner/binding and step outcomes separate from final act acceptance. Loopback authentication, Origin rejection, secret-only credential output and isolated hosted bearer custody remain explicit. No source execution, credential/provider/private-log/browser inspection occurred.\n\nSimplification: One retained envelope with separate native operation outcomes is clearer than conflating preparation and submission. Semantic list generations and explicit mode recipes remove contradictions while preserving full functionality. Keep all later OAuth, Tasks/Apps/resources/prompts and cold-host application acceptance owned.\n\nWire revision repair: Section7 now distinguishes the2026 per-request metadata/server discovery/subscriptions/resultType interface from2025 initialization/session compatibility. Root rechecked the official2026 changelog and2025 transport specification. This is design correspondence, not implementation conformance; W7 must still drive pinned real clients over both transports. Primary sources: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/docs/specification/2026-07-28/changelog.mdx and https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports. In the implementation, legacy sessions/GET/DELETE are chosen supported features, not universal mandatory behavior of every compliant legacy server.\n\nCoverage: Two independent peers FULL-read primary74218B894LF SHA4545c1a406f0dc870d49ff8e333d8aaab76dffa41cad5a2ed326107074fa08ba and U370427B725LF SHA3c67de5fd64a52c3e2eef970c117adb0a6e57f92325868bd268542484c9d1992 from1c802763, plus priorRoot review. Root FULL-read both finals/audits and independently rechecked native bytes, hashes and contiguous EOF spans. Root FULL-read the index U32478B SHA1c0ac190fc9899e26c4e75fb06e5442e4d6c4d0c31309a0f8b90c268cacf6e48; unchanged715KiB index is not claimed FULL-approved. Native client prepare.ts FULL2523B, relevant derive/core and text-bound fragments corroborate findings; partial whole-file coverage is explicit. Witnesses are prospective, no Source/tests/build/gate execution.\n\nNormal review binds only the design request2c3832 / promise059afc and both exact artifacts. No implementation, cloud deployment, protocol amendment or broad parent is approved. Repair the design under the same request/promise if performer and scope remain unchanged, then publish a new exact-head review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0ff0bf7719a193fc5ef65d3aade9dbbe76046f7b","binding":"assigned","head":"9aa8fccdd66ee518082468bad526c2411048c9be","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9912ced7e190a8401d7bfd0b327952cbcf549bc9\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:059afc6810129b357bbab51774fc34657da45ac5\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:967eb2fcba817e602824d646314f4b1d8ee48f43","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}